  "summary": "Traditional Korean mask dance performance with distinctive visual characterizations reflecting social satire.",
  "description": "Features brightly painted wooden masks with exaggerated facial expressions representing various social classes and archetypes. Developed as folk entertainment with satirical commentary on Confucian society and human nature.",
  "culturalContext": "Talchum — Korea's mask-dance tradition (Hahoe byeongsin talchum UNESCO-listed 2022) — carved alder masks with hanging jaws, bulging eyes and bent noses for satire: yangban scholars mocked, monks lechered, commoners liberated in dance. Hahoe masks (national treasures) follow a documented carving-rite tradition; the mask aesthetic — exaggerated but warm — anchors Korean folk performance identity and international mask-craft exhibitions.",
